macOS: load Apple's Java 6 through the JavaVM umbrella framework

On Mac OS X 10.5 and 10.6 the only JRE available is Apple's own Java 6, which
is also what the community PowerPC port descends from. None of the paths tried
by _load_jvm() find it:

  - it has no libjli.dylib at all (it predates the jli launcher split), so
    _load_jli_macos() always fails;
  - /usr/libexec/java_home does exist and answers
    /System/Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/1.6.0.jdk/Contents/Home, but there
    is no lib/server or jre/lib/server underneath it: the VM libraries sit in
    ../Libraries;
  - and those libraries export only JNI_CreateJavaVM_Impl, so pointing dlopen()
    straight at them would not help either.

The JavaVM umbrella framework is what exports JNI_CreateJavaVM and dispatches to
the VM matching the process architecture, which also side-steps libjvm.dylib
being i386-only. Verified on 10.6.8 with Apple Java 1.6.0_65: dlopen() of the
framework followed by JNI_CreateJavaVM starts a VM in both i386 and x86_64.

Tried last, so a modern Oracle/OpenJDK install still wins where one exists.

--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
@@ -233,9 +233,20 @@
 #endif
 
 #ifdef __APPLE__
+#include <dlfcn.h>
 // The current official JRE is installed by Oracle's Java Applet internet plugin:
 #define MACOS_JRE_HOME "/Library/Internet Plug-Ins/JavaAppletPlugin.plugin/Contents/Home"
 static const char *jre_plugin_path = MACOS_JRE_HOME;
+// Apple's own Java 6, the last JRE available on Mac OS X 10.5 and 10.6 (and the
+// base of the community PowerPC port), predates both the jli launcher and the
+// jre/lib/server layout, so every path tried above misses it. Its VM libraries
+// live in <jdk>/Contents/Libraries and export only JNI_CreateJavaVM_Impl: the
+// umbrella framework is what exports JNI_CreateJavaVM and dispatches to the VM
+// matching the process architecture (libjvm.dylib is i386-only, libserver.dylib
+// is universal). Opened with dlopen() rather than dl_dlopen(), which would
+// append a .dylib suffix the framework binary does not have.
+#define MACOS_JAVAVM_FRAMEWORK \
+    "/System/Library/Frameworks/JavaVM.framework/Versions/A/JavaVM"
 #endif
 
 #if defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(HAVE_BDJ_J2ME)
@@ -480,6 +491,13 @@
     handle = _jvm_dlopen(jre_plugin_path, "lib/server", jvm_lib);
     if (handle) {
         *p_java_home = jre_plugin_path;
+        return handle;
+    }
+    // Apple's Java 6 (10.5 / 10.6): load the umbrella framework directly.
+    BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ, "Opening %s ...\n", MACOS_JAVAVM_FRAMEWORK);
+    handle = dlopen(MACOS_JAVAVM_FRAMEWORK, RTLD_NOW | RTLD_LOCAL);
+    if (handle) {
+        *p_java_home = java_home ? java_home : MACOS_JRE_HOME;
         return handle;
     }
 #endif
